======================================================================
BONFERRONI ORDER: threshold 1/14 (7-wall) vs gap 2/25
======================================================================

beta = 1/14 (threshold 1/14); danger per runner = 0.1429; S_1 = 12*0.1429 = 1.7143
   AP {1..12}: S1=1.714 S2=1.972 S3=3.852 S4=7.251
      IE partials 1-S1(+S2)(-S3)(+S4): -0.714 1.258 -2.595 4.656   [true safe=0.0341]
   doubled-apex: S1=1.714 S2=1.866 S3=3.425 S4=6.197
      IE partials 1-S1(+S2)(-S3)(+S4): -0.714 1.151 -2.274 3.923   [true safe=0.0395]

beta = 2/25 (gap 2/25); danger per runner = 0.1600; S_1 = 12*0.1600 = 1.9200
   AP {1..12}: S1=1.920 S2=2.291 S3=4.315 S4=8.121
      IE partials 1-S1(+S2)(-S3)(+S4): -0.920 1.371 -2.944 5.177   [true safe=0.0000]
   doubled-apex: S1=1.920 S2=2.176 S3=3.847 S4=6.941
      IE partials 1-S1(+S2)(-S3)(+S4): -0.920 1.256 -2.591 4.350   [true safe=0.0000]

READING: at beta=1/14 the pair term S_2 already dominates (7-wall closes ~c<=7);
at beta=2/25 the alternating IE partials swing WIDE -- covering needs high-order
resonance (the AP's additive energy), so no low-order Bonferroni certificate exists.
This is WHY the gap (G) is strictly harder than the LRC(14) threshold.
